And he said it was one of the things he took when the office wrapped and he has it to this day.

508
00:22:58.470 --> 00:22:59.550
That is pretty cool.

509
00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:01.230
He said it's really odd looking.

510
00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:04.630
But he was like, I have to have it.

511
00:23:05.030 --> 00:23:13.930
This is reminding me of my pen with the wonky broken giraffe on the end of it on like a little broken spring.

512
00:23:14.170 --> 00:23:14.450
Yeah.

513
00:23:14.590 --> 00:23:18.890
That was in my pencil cup and I still have it in my pencil cup at home now.

514
00:23:19.110 --> 00:23:19.330
Yeah.

515
00:23:19.850 --> 00:23:21.170
Just some odd trinket.

516
00:23:21.230 --> 00:23:22.030
Some odd trinket.

517
00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:25.570
I have the cat paper clip holder that has the chipped ear.

518
00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:27.310
It's on my desk from day one.

519
00:23:27.310 --> 00:23:33.570
I wish that I had that weird, like, you remember it?

520
00:23:33.730 --> 00:23:38.270
We talked about it a long time ago when we were breaking down our desks like back in season one.

521
00:23:38.470 --> 00:23:46.430
It was like a plastic colorful thing that had like spiky.

522
00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:47.250
Oh, yeah.

523
00:23:47.370 --> 00:23:48.370
Things coming out of it.

524
00:23:48.630 --> 00:23:48.750
Yeah.

525
00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:49.410
Different colors.

526
00:23:49.630 --> 00:23:52.470
Different colored plastic things on it.

527
00:23:52.630 --> 00:23:53.530
I mean, wow.

528
00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:55.010
Could I be describing it worse?

529
00:23:55.150 --> 00:23:55.990
I don't think so.

530
00:23:57.010 --> 00:23:59.110
But I wish I had that thing.

531
00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:00.970
But it did eventually disappear.

532
00:24:00.970 --> 00:24:04.510
Like when Pam took it to Michael's paper company, it was in her box.

533
00:24:04.510 --> 00:24:06.090
But I don't think it ever returned.

534
00:24:06.430 --> 00:24:12.050
Well, at one minute and 25 seconds, Creed is playing the guitar in his office for some women.

535
00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:16.030
I was wondering if these women had a name, if they were described at all.

536
00:24:16.370 --> 00:24:22.150
All I could find in the shooting draft is that Creed tells Jordan his friend Ace is stopping by.

537
00:24:22.910 --> 00:24:23.270
Hmm.

538
00:24:23.550 --> 00:24:25.230
Not sure this lady looks like an ace.

539
00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:29.830
I asked Creed about this scene and he said, actually, there were two women.

540
00:24:30.090 --> 00:24:30.850
One is dancing.

541
00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:31.590
One is seated.

542
00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:37.750
They were both dressed kind of like hippies and that they just told Creed to play his guitar and that's his actual guitar.

543
00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:38.930
And he's actually playing.

544
00:24:39.690 --> 00:24:39.970
Nice.

545
00:24:40.190 --> 00:24:42.230
I guess Creed just has friends stop by.

546
00:24:43.610 --> 00:24:46.410
Those two women, they were just background performers.

547
00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:48.630
That was a great day of work.

548
00:24:48.850 --> 00:24:51.790
I mean, you get to just sit in an office and listen to Creed play guitar.

549
00:24:52.010 --> 00:24:52.310
Mm-hmm.

550
00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:53.890
That's kind of awesome.

551
00:24:54.230 --> 00:24:54.410
Yeah.

552
00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:56.650
I love every time Creed played his guitar.

553
00:24:57.430 --> 00:25:00.970
Well, now we're going to go into the conference room and we're going to meet our first applicant.

554
00:25:01.430 --> 00:25:03.110
His name is Fred Henry.

555
00:25:03.490 --> 00:25:04.870
A man with two first names.

556
00:25:05.950 --> 00:25:07.070
Oh, good point.

557
00:25:07.290 --> 00:25:07.470
Mm-hmm.

558
00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:09.810
Fred Henry was played by Will Arnett.

559
00:25:10.110 --> 00:25:11.950
Will kind of talked a little bit about this scene.

560
00:25:12.470 --> 00:25:19.550
I love it that he has a three-step plan to double their profits, but, you know, he's only going to share the plan if he gets the job.

561
00:25:19.690 --> 00:25:22.010
And then they're like, well, how do we know you have a plan?

562
00:25:22.290 --> 00:25:22.650
Mm-hmm.

563
00:25:23.350 --> 00:25:26.450
And then he's like, I wouldn't tell you I have a plan if I didn't have a plan.

564
00:25:26.970 --> 00:25:28.130
And then he shares.

565
00:25:28.570 --> 00:25:30.470
Just one, one step of the plan.

566
00:25:30.650 --> 00:25:31.930
One part of one step.

567
00:25:32.150 --> 00:25:34.030
Which is color code said documents.

568
00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:34.470
TM.

569
00:25:35.090 --> 00:25:35.270
Yeah.

570
00:25:36.450 --> 00:25:37.630
I wanted to discuss.

571
00:25:38.590 --> 00:25:41.890
I think he's right to not share his plan to double their profits.

572
00:25:42.690 --> 00:25:43.170
Mm-hmm.

573
00:25:43.310 --> 00:25:44.510
Why would he do that?

574
00:25:44.970 --> 00:25:45.970
I mean, I get it.

575
00:25:46.090 --> 00:25:48.490
They're like, but we need, like, are you the real deal?

576
00:25:48.770 --> 00:25:50.190
Because then they could just take his idea.

577
00:25:50.310 --> 00:25:51.270
They don't need to hire him.

578
00:25:51.430 --> 00:25:51.710
Of course.

579
00:25:51.850 --> 00:25:51.990
Yeah.

580
00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:52.570
Yes.

581
00:25:52.890 --> 00:26:00.070
You know, this is a complaint of a lot of writers in Hollywood, which is that they'll get these meetings for a different show.

582
00:26:00.070 --> 00:26:06.110
And then part of your meeting is that you sit down and pitch ideas for this television show or this movie.

583
00:26:07.050 --> 00:26:09.110
But it's not, you don't own your ideas.

584
00:26:09.270 --> 00:26:12.030
Like, once you give them in the interview, they can just, like, not hire you.

585
00:26:12.110 --> 00:26:14.090
But they can be like, those are a couple of good ideas.

586
00:26:14.310 --> 00:26:14.470
Yeah.

587
00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:16.470
I mean, it makes sense to me.

588
00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:18.810
When is it your intellectual property and when is it not?

589
00:26:19.290 --> 00:26:22.050
Lady, I one time auditioned for a show.

590
00:26:22.250 --> 00:26:22.430
Yeah.

591
00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:25.010
And I improvised a line in my audition.

592
00:26:25.250 --> 00:26:25.430
Yeah.

593
00:26:25.510 --> 00:26:26.570
And I did not get the part.

594
00:26:26.570 --> 00:26:29.530
And the line I improvised when I watched the show was in it.

595
00:26:29.670 --> 00:26:30.650
This is what I'm saying.

596
00:26:31.050 --> 00:26:31.690
I don't know.

597
00:26:31.770 --> 00:26:33.730
I think if they wanted the line, they need to hire you.

598
00:26:33.930 --> 00:26:34.410
I know.

599
00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:37.350
If they want your good idea, then you need to get the job.

600
00:26:37.530 --> 00:26:38.450
That's how I feel about it.

601
00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:40.450
You heard it from me.

602
00:26:40.610 --> 00:26:41.310
I'm with you.

603
00:26:41.310 --> 00:26:47.010
So, Jim's going to have a talking head where he says, we are the search committee.

604
00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:49.850
And the search committee is him and Toby and Gabe.

605
00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:52.330
And they're looking for their candidates.

606
00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:55.310
And Jim did not know that Gabe's last name was Lewis.

607
00:26:55.850 --> 00:26:57.490
Hey, two first names again.

608
00:26:57.770 --> 00:26:58.730
Gabe Lewis.

609
00:26:59.010 --> 00:26:59.390
Oh.

610
00:27:00.010 --> 00:27:03.210
You know, my husband has two first names or two last names.

611
00:27:03.490 --> 00:27:04.150
That's true.

612
00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:05.130
Lee Kirk.

613
00:27:05.490 --> 00:27:06.570
Have we talked about this?

614
00:27:06.710 --> 00:27:06.850
Yes.

615
00:27:06.850 --> 00:27:08.970
And sometimes you go places and they're like, Kirk Lee?

616
00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:09.690
Always.

617
00:27:10.010 --> 00:27:10.230
Always.

618
00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:11.290
50% of the time.

619
00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:12.730
Hello, Mr. Lee.

