There is no such thing as a product.

1026
00:44:41.000 --> 00:44:42.560
Don't ever think there is.

1027
00:44:43.480 --> 00:44:45.940
There is only sex.

1028
00:44:47.100 --> 00:44:48.800
Everything is sex.

1029
00:44:49.740 --> 00:44:55.380
You understand that what I'm telling you is a universal truth, Toby.

1030
00:44:55.800 --> 00:44:56.260
Yes.

1031
00:44:57.320 --> 00:44:59.720
I mean, I hung on his every word.

1032
00:44:59.860 --> 00:45:02.440
I mean, the scene continues and it's so rich.

1033
00:45:02.680 --> 00:45:03.240
Yes.

1034
00:45:03.240 --> 00:45:05.660
We had a fan question from Susanna H.

1035
00:45:05.880 --> 00:45:07.680
in Clermont, Florida, who said,

1036
00:45:07.820 --> 00:45:16.660
did they pick James Spader and then write Robert California for him to play or did they create Robert California and cast James Spader because he's perfect for it?

1037
00:45:16.660 --> 00:45:18.360
I kind of mentioned this.

1038
00:45:18.800 --> 00:45:26.920
They created Robert California first, and then Paul had the idea of James Spader to play this role.

1039
00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:28.680
It is perfect.

1040
00:45:29.280 --> 00:45:30.320
I loved it.

1041
00:45:30.320 --> 00:45:40.600
I know for certain that James Spader memorized that word for word because later in season eight, when we go on to work with him, he was like a word for word actor.

1042
00:45:41.520 --> 00:45:43.660
It's exactly as it's scripted.

1043
00:45:43.820 --> 00:45:44.040
Yes.

1044
00:45:44.220 --> 00:45:48.280
And he talked about someone who just has such a presence.

1045
00:45:48.900 --> 00:45:55.120
And Paul and Zach and John's reactions as their characters to him are so great, too.

1046
00:45:55.420 --> 00:45:57.360
They are terrified of him.

1047
00:45:57.500 --> 00:45:58.980
They're mesmerized by him.

1048
00:45:59.140 --> 00:46:02.620
And then his like look to camera at the end.

1049
00:46:02.620 --> 00:46:07.880
And yeah, Paul said James immediately knew how to put a button to the camera.

1050
00:46:07.920 --> 00:46:08.820
And it was pretty amazing.

1051
00:46:09.520 --> 00:46:12.060
Well, a great line for anyone in an interview.

1052
00:46:12.300 --> 00:46:20.220
If anyone ever says to you, you know, we think you might be overqualified, just look right at him and say, do I look like someone that would waste my own time?

1053
00:46:20.920 --> 00:46:21.880
It's amazing.

1054
00:46:22.700 --> 00:46:24.320
Well, his interview is done.

1055
00:46:24.520 --> 00:46:32.720
Robert California is going to exit the conference room and he sizes up the whole bullpen and you can feel his gaze like it affects everybody.

1056
00:46:33.440 --> 00:46:36.200
Jim says that he creeps him out, but he might just be a genius.

1057
00:46:37.360 --> 00:46:39.360
And now it's time for Daryl's interview.

1058
00:46:40.220 --> 00:46:43.560
But Daryl, I think he thinks he's just going to get the job.

1059
00:46:43.680 --> 00:46:45.040
He has not prepared a resume.

1060
00:46:45.540 --> 00:46:49.360
This is very un-Daryl, like because in the warehouse, he's so prepared.

1061
00:46:49.560 --> 00:46:50.680
But I think you're right.

1062
00:46:50.680 --> 00:46:52.220
I think he thought he was a shoo-in.

1063
00:46:52.340 --> 00:46:53.460
Like this is a formality.

1064
00:46:53.720 --> 00:46:53.900
Yeah.

1065
00:46:54.420 --> 00:46:58.420
We got a lot of mail about this storyline with Daryl.

1066
00:46:58.820 --> 00:47:06.100
And kind of like you said about how you kind of can't believe, it seems out of character that Daryl wasn't more prepared.

1067
00:47:06.480 --> 00:47:07.740
This was a criticism.

1068
00:47:08.180 --> 00:47:15.480
All I could think of was maybe he's never had to do an interview, like maybe at the warehouse he was hired and worked his way up or something.

1069
00:47:16.080 --> 00:47:18.080
But it didn't track for me.

1070
00:47:18.080 --> 00:47:23.040
It didn't track with a lot of fans, and it also didn't track with Miles McNutt at the A.V.

1071
00:47:23.140 --> 00:47:23.420
Club.

1072
00:47:23.580 --> 00:47:24.240
Oh, Miles.

1073
00:47:24.480 --> 00:47:29.820
This was one of his main criticisms of this episode, which he otherwise scored as a B-.

1074
00:47:30.360 --> 00:47:30.840
Okay.

1075
00:47:30.840 --> 00:47:37.520
He said, quote, while I can understand Daryl expecting that he would get the job based on his strong relationships with people,

1076
00:47:37.700 --> 00:47:42.320
the idea of not even bothering with a resume seems to reduce his intelligence too far.

1077
00:47:42.500 --> 00:47:46.660
He can still have hubris without seeming entirely unprofessional.

1078
00:47:47.020 --> 00:47:49.860
And I don't even think it was worth it for a clippy joke.

1079
00:47:50.060 --> 00:47:51.440
He's not wrong, Miles.

1080
00:47:51.560 --> 00:47:52.200
You're not wrong.

1081
00:47:52.320 --> 00:47:53.040
You're not wrong.

1082
00:47:53.100 --> 00:47:54.260
I kind of agree.

1083
00:47:54.260 --> 00:47:57.180
This felt a little too far out of character.

1084
00:47:57.520 --> 00:48:02.320
We are now down in Caffeine Corner for one of my favorite scenes of the whole episode.

1085
00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:09.480
Dwight is in the corner, and he is watching an exchange between Merv Bronte and Robert California.

1086
00:48:09.760 --> 00:48:12.440
Merv Bronte, played by Ray Romano.

1087
00:48:12.660 --> 00:48:14.420
He's coming in for his interview.

1088
00:48:14.420 --> 00:48:21.400
As perfect as James Spader was for Robert California, I thought Ray Romano was perfection for Merv Bronte.

1089
00:48:21.780 --> 00:48:26.800
Yeah, Merv just sort of says, like, oh, you know, just how is it?

1090
00:48:26.900 --> 00:48:28.160
He's making small talk.

1091
00:48:28.280 --> 00:48:28.520
Yes.

1092
00:48:28.760 --> 00:48:38.100
And Robert California, because he is just mischievous and somewhat sinister, plants a seed of doubt.

1093
00:48:38.260 --> 00:48:42.920
He's like, you know, that just looks like a place where everyone is just working till they die.

1094
00:48:42.920 --> 00:48:44.980
It's, like, dreadful up there.

1095
00:48:45.080 --> 00:48:45.800
It's horrible.

1096
00:48:46.680 --> 00:48:49.980
And Merv gets in the elevator, and Robert is in Merv's head.

1097
00:48:50.120 --> 00:48:54.200
Merv gets in the elevator, and he's like, no, I don't even know if I want this job.

1098
00:48:54.640 --> 00:48:55.120
Yeah.

1099
00:48:55.440 --> 00:49:00.200
Well, Dwight doesn't care for what Robert California has said about Dunder Mifflin.

1100
00:49:00.320 --> 00:49:03.520
And he's like, what qualifies you to make these judgments?

1101
00:49:04.000 --> 00:49:07.100
And Robert California just stares him down.

1102
00:49:07.380 --> 00:49:08.760
And Dwight's like, stop it.

1103
00:49:09.600 --> 00:49:11.740
Stop trying to figure me out.

1104
00:49:11.820 --> 00:49:12.600
I already figured you out.

1105
00:49:12.920 --> 00:49:13.420
Oh.

1106
00:49:13.700 --> 00:49:14.140
It's already done.

1107
00:49:14.380 --> 00:49:17.560
I mean, I loved the two of them going toe-to-toe here.

1108
00:49:18.100 --> 00:49:19.520
How fun for Rain.

1109
00:49:19.700 --> 00:49:21.420
That must have been so fun as an actor.

1110
00:49:21.680 --> 00:49:22.200
Yes.

1111
00:49:22.420 --> 00:49:22.600
Yeah.

1112
00:49:22.920 --> 00:49:31.180
I mean, I feel like also, of all the applicants, we see Robert California interact with other office members.

1113
00:49:31.540 --> 00:49:31.720
Yes.

1114
00:49:31.820 --> 00:49:34.660
You know, mostly the applicants are just with the search committee.

1115
00:49:34.660 --> 00:49:36.400
But I have to imagine.

1116
00:49:36.580 --> 00:49:41.000
I know that Paul said we had no plans to bring James Spader back for season eight.

1117
00:49:41.000 --> 00:49:49.060
But I have to imagine that when they got in the editing room and they saw this scene between Robert California and Dwight Schrute, I mean, I just, as a writer,

1118
00:49:49.180 --> 00:49:51.580
I would have begun having like a flurry of ideas.

1119
00:49:51.580 --> 00:49:52.600
It's so great.

1120
00:49:52.980 --> 00:49:55.100
How he affects everybody is so great.

1121
00:49:55.240 --> 00:49:57.200
And of course, this is a plot point.

1122
00:49:57.360 --> 00:49:59.540
We need to motivate Dwight to reapply.

1123
00:49:59.720 --> 00:50:00.100
That's right.

1124
00:50:00.100 --> 00:50:03.080
And now Dwight is like, I'm not going to let this guy get the job.

1125
00:50:03.080 --> 00:50:04.480
And he's going to reapply.

1126
00:50:05.060 --> 00:50:08.000
Merv, meanwhile, is going to have a sandwich during his interview.

1127
00:50:08.400 --> 00:50:10.800
He has a line that made me laugh so hard.

1128
00:50:10.960 --> 00:50:14.960
He says, they ask him why he left his other job.

1129
00:50:15.060 --> 00:50:18.160
And he says, I'm leaving my other job because they were all jerks, really.

1130
00:50:18.440 --> 00:50:21.460
Then he opens up his Tupperware and he starts eating a sandwich.

1131
00:50:21.460 --> 00:50:25.940
And he says, you know, all of them, you had your jerk wads and your jerk offs.

1132
00:50:26.140 --> 00:50:28.860
So just between the wads and the offs, I had to get out of there.

1133
00:50:29.440 --> 00:50:34.060
I also noticed how Ray Romano, he didn't bite into his sandwich.

1134
00:50:34.280 --> 00:50:35.980
He tore off a piece of sandwich.

1135
00:50:36.420 --> 00:50:38.740
Do you bite your food or do you tear?

1136
00:50:39.040 --> 00:50:41.280
Because it's a way to eat your sandwich.

1137
00:50:41.680 --> 00:50:42.640
I mean, here's the thing.

1138
00:50:42.760 --> 00:50:44.360
We don't really know Ray Romano.

1139
00:50:44.540 --> 00:50:47.200
Maybe he normally eats a sandwich by just biting into it.

1140
00:50:47.320 --> 00:50:53.720
But I love the character choice that this man, Merv, is like all of a sudden annoyed.

1141
00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:55.060
Annoyed that he's there.

1142
00:50:55.220 --> 00:50:58.640
So him like tearing at it, talking about the jerks and the jerk wads.

1143
00:50:58.860 --> 00:51:01.020
It just fit for me for the story.

1144
00:51:01.840 --> 00:51:04.320
But yeah, I generally bite into a sandwich.

1145
00:51:04.600 --> 00:51:09.140
I think I'm going to put this in my acting journal that I keep of behaviors.

1146
00:51:09.840 --> 00:51:15.980
You know, remember I told you one of mine is like lady who applies lipstick immediately after finishing a meal.

1147
00:51:16.180 --> 00:51:16.360
Yeah.

1148
00:51:16.580 --> 00:51:17.740
That's like a character trait.

1149
00:51:18.080 --> 00:51:19.940
One of mine is nervous laughter lady.

1150
00:51:20.420 --> 00:51:20.820
Yes.

1151
00:51:21.040 --> 00:51:21.200
Yes.

1152
00:51:21.320 --> 00:51:22.720
That she's like, how are you doing?

1153
00:51:24.940 --> 00:51:25.340
Yes.

1154
00:51:25.780 --> 00:51:32.020
And like then when I get a role, I go to my little journal and I see if any of these little quirks apply to my character.

1155
00:51:32.920 --> 00:51:37.980
Well, I'm going to have to put down person who picks sandwich instead of bites into it.

1156
00:51:37.980 --> 00:51:40.160
Or bring sandwich to a meeting.

1157
00:51:41.300 --> 00:51:41.460
Yeah.

1158
00:51:41.800 --> 00:51:41.940
Yeah.

1159
00:51:42.640 --> 00:51:46.240
Jenna, I was in a movie called Struck by Lightning and I was the high school counselor.

1160
00:51:46.560 --> 00:51:48.460
The director was Brian Dannelly.

1161
00:51:48.780 --> 00:51:50.060
And we were talking about this scene.

1162
00:51:50.180 --> 00:51:53.120
It's between my character and Chris Colfer, who's a high school student.

1163
00:51:53.240 --> 00:51:53.340
Yeah.

1164
00:51:53.940 --> 00:51:55.540
Chris Colfer actually wrote the script.

1165
00:51:55.960 --> 00:52:01.320
And Chris and Brian and I were talking about like, what would my character be doing as students were coming in to talk to her?

1166
00:52:01.420 --> 00:52:03.740
And they were saying, well, maybe she took this meeting on her lunch break.

1167
00:52:04.040 --> 00:52:06.860
She's eating a sandwich, like through the whole scene.

1168
00:52:07.020 --> 00:52:08.580
And I was like, oh, yeah, that'd be great.

1169
00:52:08.700 --> 00:52:10.580
And we decided on a big sloppy sandwich.

1170
00:52:11.360 --> 00:52:14.440
And it was so difficult to eat it and talk at the same time.

1171
00:52:14.580 --> 00:52:16.040
Things were falling out of the sandwich.

1172
00:52:16.220 --> 00:52:17.460
It was a hot mess.

1173
00:52:17.820 --> 00:52:19.340
It made it into the bloopers.

1174
00:52:19.640 --> 00:52:24.400
So Jenna, for your actor's journal, I'm just saying, be particular about the sandwich.

1175
00:52:24.400 --> 00:52:27.020
Do I want a large sloppy sandwich?

1176
00:52:27.380 --> 00:52:28.300
Maybe I do.

1177
00:52:28.680 --> 00:52:30.420
It sounds like it was a hit.

1178
00:52:31.180 --> 00:52:35.480
Difficult to deal with in the moment, but provided some good comedy.

1179
00:52:35.800 --> 00:52:39.880
I can put the bloopers in our stories and you tell me if it was the right choice.

1180
00:52:40.480 --> 00:52:43.820
Well, we have Erin sure to thank for that Ray Romano cameo.

1181
00:52:44.080 --> 00:52:47.360
You remember that Erin was a writer on Everybody Loves Raymond.

1182
00:52:47.620 --> 00:52:47.860
Yes.

1183
00:52:48.020 --> 00:52:51.260
And Erin volunteered to reach out to Ray Romano.

1184
00:52:51.420 --> 00:52:52.620
And Ray said yes immediately.

1185
00:52:52.620 --> 00:52:55.620
I loved, loved Ray Romano as Merv.

1186
00:52:56.240 --> 00:53:00.420
Angela has returned to the office in a chauffeured car.

1187
00:53:00.720 --> 00:53:01.240
Yes.

1188
00:53:01.380 --> 00:53:07.600
You guys, in the extended cut, Angela shows Hank her ring first before everyone else.

1189
00:53:07.740 --> 00:53:08.980
Her giant ring?

1190
00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:09.480
Yes.

1191
00:53:09.620 --> 00:53:11.320
What, is she Naomi Judd?

1192
00:53:11.420 --> 00:53:12.080
I know.

1193
00:53:12.260 --> 00:53:14.240
And Hank goes, damn, girl.

1194
00:53:14.540 --> 00:53:15.780
And I say, I know.

1195
00:53:16.400 --> 00:53:20.100
And then she goes up to the office and shows it to the bullpen.

1196
00:53:20.540 --> 00:53:21.900
So here's the thing about this ring.

1197
00:53:21.900 --> 00:53:27.440
I remember Phil Shea coming over to me with a box of rings and they wanted it to be a

1198
00:53:27.440 --> 00:53:28.160
showstopper.

1199
00:53:28.300 --> 00:53:31.840
They wanted it to be a ring that Kelly would be impressed with.

1200
00:53:32.020 --> 00:53:32.980
That's a good note.

1201
00:53:33.420 --> 00:53:38.240
So it was about like three carats with little diamonds on either side.

1202
00:53:38.460 --> 00:53:41.300
I think it was a cushion cut or emerald cut.

1203
00:53:41.440 --> 00:53:41.780
I can't remember.

1204
00:53:41.860 --> 00:53:42.880
It was like one of those, right?

1205
00:53:42.980 --> 00:53:43.880
Like the square rectangle.

1206
00:53:44.120 --> 00:53:45.400
But it was really big.

1207
00:53:45.960 --> 00:53:51.000
Jenna, I Googled what rings that size cost and they are so expensive.

1208
00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:51.600
Oh, yeah.

1209
00:53:51.900 --> 00:53:53.020
I had no idea.

1210
00:53:53.600 --> 00:53:53.980
Yeah.

1211
00:53:54.200 --> 00:53:55.460
Your state senator.

1212
00:53:55.660 --> 00:53:56.340
I don't know.

1213
00:53:56.340 --> 00:53:58.000
He threw down some cash for that.

1214
00:53:58.360 --> 00:53:58.620
Yeah.

1215
00:53:59.100 --> 00:54:04.380
Something I didn't mention from the cliffhangers document and the seven pages of pitches that

1216
00:54:04.380 --> 00:54:10.960
involved your character marrying the senator is that in the end was there were several pitches

1217
00:54:10.960 --> 00:54:14.200
where we were going to find out that the senator was corrupt.

1218
00:54:14.780 --> 00:54:15.020
Oh.

1219
00:54:15.360 --> 00:54:19.080
And you were going to have to deal with whether or not you turn him in.

1220
00:54:19.620 --> 00:54:21.520
So he's getting money from somewhere.

1221
00:54:21.660 --> 00:54:24.200
I mean, see, they planted the seed with this ring.

1222
00:54:24.360 --> 00:54:25.500
How does he have this money?

1223
00:54:25.680 --> 00:54:26.360
I don't know.

1224
00:54:26.480 --> 00:54:27.420
Maybe it's a fake ring.

1225
00:54:27.780 --> 00:54:30.340
Well, Angela starts to tell their engagement story.

1226
00:54:30.740 --> 00:54:37.100
She says that he tucked a flower behind her ear and he got down on one knee to ask her

1227
00:54:37.100 --> 00:54:38.360
to be a senator's wife.

1228
00:54:38.520 --> 00:54:39.400
And Pam laughs.

1229
00:54:39.400 --> 00:54:42.880
And I'm saying right here and now that this is some Pam snark.

1230
00:54:43.040 --> 00:54:44.420
She couldn't help herself.

1231
00:54:44.460 --> 00:54:47.080
She said he talked about himself in third person.

1232
00:54:47.620 --> 00:54:52.000
And Angela's like, yes, Pam, not everyone is as informal as you and Jim.

1233
00:54:52.200 --> 00:54:52.900
Dude, whatever.

1234
00:54:53.100 --> 00:54:53.860
Want to marry me?

1235
00:54:54.180 --> 00:54:55.320
And everybody laughs.

1236
00:54:55.760 --> 00:54:57.180
Pam's like, that's not how it happened.

1237
00:54:57.420 --> 00:55:01.240
And then one of my favorite things is Angela says, and then I saw flashes.

1238
00:55:01.880 --> 00:55:02.740
Reporters were there.

1239
00:55:02.960 --> 00:55:03.900
They always find us.

1240
00:55:04.140 --> 00:55:06.280
And everyone was crying, even his aide.

1241
00:55:06.280 --> 00:55:10.900
And Oscar's going to have a talking head where he says Angela is engaged to a gay man.

1242
00:55:11.000 --> 00:55:15.000
And he says as a gay man and as Angela's friend, he's horrified.

1243
00:55:15.000 --> 00:55:17.300
But as a lover of elegant weddings, he's excited.

1244
00:55:17.680 --> 00:55:20.760
So Oscar has decided you're going to have a nice wedding.

1245
00:55:20.900 --> 00:55:21.940
He's pretty sure.

1246
00:55:22.240 --> 00:55:25.120
And he also finally can't hold on to this information anymore.

1247
00:55:25.340 --> 00:55:29.500
He needs to let people know that Angela's engaged to a gay man.

1248
00:55:29.820 --> 00:55:32.400
Guess who is going to interview for this job now?

1249
00:55:32.800 --> 00:55:33.660
It's Andy.

1250
00:55:33.660 --> 00:55:38.020
And listen, if you ever need anyone to give you a pep talk, don't ask Phyllis.

1251
00:55:38.860 --> 00:55:40.640
Because this is what she says.

1252
00:55:40.820 --> 00:55:42.460
Are you sure this is a good idea?

1253
00:55:42.660 --> 00:55:44.160
I hate to see you disappointed.

1254
00:55:44.860 --> 00:55:45.340
Yeah.

1255
00:55:45.800 --> 00:55:49.440
Well, right off the bat in his interview, Gabe is not having it.

1256
00:55:50.100 --> 00:55:50.580
No.

1257
00:55:50.960 --> 00:55:55.440
He's just sort of throwing out odd, unrelated questions.

1258
00:55:55.540 --> 00:55:57.940
How many windows are there in New York City?

1259
00:55:57.940 --> 00:56:06.920
But then Andy throws down some serious knowledge about the sun, which causes Gabe to flip out and scream, shut up about the sun.

1260
00:56:08.200 --> 00:56:08.980
Emma M.

1261
00:56:09.080 --> 00:56:12.100
in Florida wanted to know if that was scripted or improvised.

1262
00:56:12.440 --> 00:56:14.200
Emma, that was scripted.

1263
00:56:14.480 --> 00:56:14.880
Yeah.

1264
00:56:15.140 --> 00:56:16.320
Shut up about the sun.

1265
00:56:16.320 --> 00:56:21.480
I feel like that would maybe make like a fun, ironic coffee mug.

1266
00:56:21.900 --> 00:56:23.340
Shut up about the sun.

1267
00:56:24.920 --> 00:56:27.020
Meanwhile, Daryl is working on his resume.

1268
00:56:27.300 --> 00:56:30.700
And as Miles McNutt said, this is the Clippy joke, right?

1269
00:56:30.960 --> 00:56:31.300
Yes.

1270
00:56:31.540 --> 00:56:32.400
Remember Clippy?

1271
00:56:32.520 --> 00:56:33.400
This is old tech.

1272
00:56:33.540 --> 00:56:34.560
It was part of Microsoft.

1273
00:56:35.140 --> 00:56:35.400
Yes.

1274
00:56:35.560 --> 00:56:38.960
When you would open a document, a little talking paper clip would come up.

1275
00:56:39.080 --> 00:56:40.100
I remember Clippy.

1276
00:56:40.340 --> 00:56:40.720
Mm-hmm.

1277
00:56:40.920 --> 00:56:42.160
I looked up Clippy.

1278
00:56:42.500 --> 00:56:44.180
Clippy's original name was Clip It.

1279
